window.helpSearch=[{"title": "Welcome to RayWn2App Help", "url": "index.html", "text": "\n Welcome to RayWn2App Help \n Welcome to RayWn2App Help \n Welcome to RayWn2App, your WN-2 station display and operating companion. Watch forward and reflected power, check SWR, arrange meters to suit your station, and keep records of your operating sessions. You can start with a ready-made layout and use the program without designing a dashboard. When you are ready, change the arrangement, add instruments and save different layouts for different activities. \n Start here \n \n Install and open RayWn2App . \n Connect your WN-2 . \n Check Device Settings , including the coupler fitted to each channel. \n Choose a layout  and start reading your meters. \n \n A radio-and-amplifier layout connected to a physical WN-2. RF is idle in this example. Select image to enlarge. \n Find your way around \n The  main menu guide  explains where to find each tool. The  Layout Edit Mode walkthrough  takes you through creating your own display.  Instruments  explains which meter to choose, including the  cross-needle meter . RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration for normal operation. Select the correct coupler model for each channel. Optional  user reference matching  is available if you want the displayed power to agree with another meter you have chosen. \n Using this Help \n Select a topic at the left. Search all Help finds topics containing your words; press Enter to open a match. Back, Forward and Home help you retrace your steps. Find in this page searches the open article. A\u2212 and A+ change the reading size; 100% restores it. Select any screenshot to open a larger view. It initially fits the window, enlarging small images. Use + and \u2212 to zoom, 100% for original size, or Fit to fill the available space without changing the image proportions. Scroll to inspect an image when zoomed in. Ctrl++ and Ctrl+\u2212 zoom; Ctrl+0 returns to original size. You can leave Help open while following the steps in the main window. F1 opens Help from the main window. In a settings or tool window, F1 opens the related topic. Plain F1 is reserved for Help; audio-message shortcuts start at F2. While entering a shortcut in a key field, keys are captured by that field instead. This guide is available without an Internet connection. Links to outside websites require an Internet connection. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "First station connection", "url": "quick-start.html", "text": "\n First station connection \n First station connection \n On this page Before connecting Connect in RayWn2App Check the idle display Save your arrangement Before connecting \n Power the WN-2 and connect its USB cable to your computer. On Windows, first install the  WaveNode USB driver for Windows 10 and 11 ; see  Windows driver setup . Begin with the transmitter in receive. Close any other program that is using the same WN-2. \n Connect in RayWn2App \n \n Press  Discover  on the toolbar below the menus. \n Open the device list and select your WN-2 serial number. The connection name may include D2XX or VCP and a COM port. \n Press  Connect . Wait for Live and a healthy link indication at the bottom of the window. \n Open  Device \u2192 Settings . Select that WN-2 and check the  RF couplers  tab. Choose the model actually connected to each channel, then press  OK . \n The untouched initial  Untitled layout  binds automatically when the first connected device supplies live data. For another arrangement, open  Layout \u2192 Station Layouts , choose Single Radio or Radio and Amplifier, select your WN-2 identity, and load it. Saved or edited layouts are not rebound automatically. \n \n The serial number identifies the connected WN-2; each instrument is assigned to one of its channels. Select image to enlarge. \n Check the idle display \n With no transmitted RF, power may read zero or a small residual value. SWR can say Low RF or Unavailable because there is not enough forward power to calculate a useful ratio. That is different from a disconnected device. The RF couplers are factory calibrated. Additional calibration is not a first-use step. Selecting the correct coupler model is important;  reference matching  is an optional later choice. \n Save your arrangement \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Save Layout As , enter a meaningful name and save. Your next session can use the same arrangement. See  Layouts  for alternatives and customization. If instruments say Device unavailable, open their Properties and compare Device identity with the connected serial number. Selecting a different toolbar device does not automatically reassign instruments. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Main window, menus and shortcuts", "url": "menus.html", "text": "\n Main window, menus and shortcuts \n Main window, menus and shortcuts \n On this page Main window at a glance Layout menu View menu Device menu Theme menu Capture menu Tools menu Diagnostics menu Help menu Main window at a glance \n The menu bar runs across the top. The device toolbar underneath selects which WN-2 the Connect and Disconnect buttons operate on. Your layout fills the main area. The bottom status line reports connection conditions. The title bar shows your layout name and, when entered, your callsign and location. An asterisk next to the layout name means there are unsaved changes. \n Menus, device toolbar, dashboard and connection status in the main window. Select image to enlarge. \n Layout menu \n Use this menu to create, open and save dashboards. \n \n Undo / Redo  reverses or restores layout edits. \n New Layout  starts an empty arrangement.  Open Layout  loads one you saved. \n Save Layout / Save Layout As  saves changes or creates a separately named copy. \n Restore Default Layout  returns to the default arrangement after checking for unsaved changes. \n Station Layouts  provides Single Radio \u2014 HF/VHF and Radio and Amplifier \u2014 HF/VHF starting points. \n Edit Layout  shows movement and resizing handles.  Add instrument  opens the instrument chooser. \n Rename Group / Duplicate Group  organizes sections.  Group columns  sets how many groups appear across the dashboard. \n \n Layout menu. See the Layout walkthrough before rearranging an important saved display. Select image to enlarge. \n View menu \n Show or hide Amplifier Gain instruments and DC voltage/current instruments. Hiding them does not delete their settings. Turn the same option on to show them again. \n Visibility controls let you simplify the display for the current activity. Select image to enlarge. \n Device menu \n Settings  opens device and application preferences.  SWR Protection ,  Overdrive Warning ,  LCD Display  and  Email Notifications  each open a separate setup window.  Open replay  is an optional diagnostic tool for reading a saved capture; it does not operate your physical WN-2. \n Device menu. Settings is the starting point for coupler selection. Select image to enlarge. \n Theme menu \n Select a color theme. This changes the appearance of the application, not your readings or layout positions. For larger or smaller controls, use  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Application \u2192 App Scale Factor . \n Theme choices are available directly from the main menu. Select image to enlarge. \n Capture menu \n Open  Scope  to inspect a captured waveform or  Envelope Spectrum  to examine its frequency content.  Stop selected device capture  stops capture on the WN-2 selected in the main toolbar. \n Scope and Envelope Spectrum are separate views of captured measurements. Select image to enlarge. \n Tools menu \n \n Station Identity  sets the callsign and location shown in the title bar. \n Speak selected WN-2  reads a channel aloud or stops spoken readings. \n Antenna Switch  opens the accessory-control panel. \n RF History and Amplifier Analysis  plots readings over time and provides recording and totals. \n Plot SWR  records SWR against frequency. \n Tone Generator ,  Audio Messages  and  Audible RF Monitor  provide audio tools. \n Thetis Telemetry  and  MQTT Telemetry  send selected station information to other software. \n Compact Station Panel  opens a small floating station display. \n \n Tools menu. Open only the tools needed for your operating activity. Select image to enlarge. \n Diagnostics menu \n USB Recovery  helps restore a troubled connection.  Live Packet Monitor  shows incoming and outgoing data.  Measurement snapshot  and  Command frame inspector  provide detailed information for troubleshooting.  WN-2 Raw Data Emulator  lets you practice without hardware.  Application Log  records application activity and errors. You do not need these diagnostic tools for ordinary meter operation.  Connection troubleshooting  is a useful first stop. \n Diagnostic tools are separate from normal station controls. Select image to enlarge. \n Help menu \n Operator help  opens this guide.  About  shows application information, licensing and acknowledgments. \n Use Help whenever you need an explanation without leaving the application. Select image to enlarge. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Callsign, location and shortcuts", "url": "station-identity.html", "text": "\n Callsign, location and shortcuts \n Callsign, location and shortcuts \n Add your callsign and location \n Open  Tools \u2192 Station Identity . Enter the callsign and location you want shown, then save. They appear in the main title bar alongside the layout name, leaving the dashboard area available for meters. \n Station identity is optional and does not change measurements or device assignments. Select image to enlarge. \n Speak a channel reading \n Select the intended WN-2 in the main device list. Choose  Tools \u2192 Speak selected WN-2 \u2192 Channel 1 , 2, 3 or 4. The corresponding application shortcuts are Ctrl+Shift+1 through Ctrl+Shift+4. Ctrl+Shift+0 stops spoken readings. These shortcuts use the toolbar's selected device, not whichever instrument you last clicked. They operate while RayWn2App has focus; Audio Messages has its own optional global hotkeys. For repeated readings or SWR tuning audio, open  Audible RF Monitor . Check the selected output and volume before using speech during another audio activity. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Preparing your WN-2 station", "url": "hardware.html", "text": "\n Preparing your WN-2 station \n Preparing your WN-2 station \n On this page Prepare the station One radio or an amplifier Factory calibration Auxiliary connections Readings and control confirmation Prepare the station \n Connect each RF coupler in the intended coax path and connect its sensor lead to the corresponding WN-2 channel. Keep track of which channel belongs to which part of your station. A clear label such as Radio output or Amplifier output is more useful than a generic meter name. Use the electrical ratings and connector labels supplied with your equipment. A larger display range does not increase a coupler's power rating. \n One radio or an amplifier \n For a simple station, use one coupler and the Single Radio layout. For amplifier gain, use one coupler before the amplifier and another after it. The supplied amplifier layout assigns CH1 to the input and CH2 to the output; change instrument assignments if your wiring differs. \n A compact starting point for a radio with one coupler. Select image to enlarge. \n Factory calibration \n RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration. Select the correct model in  Device Settings . Optional user reference matching lets the application agree with a meter of your choosing; it is not a requirement for normal operation. \n Auxiliary connections \n Temperature sensors, DC current sensing and external switches require the appropriate wiring. Do not connect relay coils directly to logic outputs. See  Temperature ,  DC and analog inputs ,  Digital I/O  and  Antenna Switch  before using those features. \n Readings and control confirmation \n A successful USB connection does not prove that an external relay moved. Check the reported device state and, when setting up external equipment, confirm its operation directly. The application identifies uncertain or unavailable readings rather than treating them as normal values. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Installing and opening RayWn2App", "url": "platforms.html", "text": "\n Installing and opening RayWn2App \n Installing and opening RayWn2App \n On this page Install RayWn2App Open the application If installation or startup fails Updates and another computer Install RayWn2App \n Use the installation package supplied with your WN-2 or obtained from RF StateSide. Choose the package for your computer and follow its instructions. The installer, or the supplied Linux installation script, installs the components needed to run RayWn2App. You do not need to install programming tools. Keep the installation instructions with the package in case you need to move to another computer. \n Choose your operating system Windows installation and USB connection Ubuntu Linux installation and USB connection Raspberry Pi installation and USB connection macOS installation and USB connection Each page includes a real connected WN-2 example and explains the driver choices for that computer. \n Open the application \n On Windows, open RayWn2App from the Start menu or its shortcut. On Linux or Raspberry Pi, use the application launcher created during installation. On macOS, open the installed application from Applications. Connect the WN-2 by USB and follow  First station connection . If the operating system asks for permission to use a microphone while you are recording messages, allow it only if you intend to record. \n If installation or startup fails \n Run the supplied installer or setup script again and follow its repair instructions. Make sure you used the package for your operating system and computer. If the problem continues, contact RF StateSide with the error message and your operating-system version. Do not change coupler calibration to resolve an installation problem. \n Updates and another computer \n Keep copies of your saved layouts and recorded messages before updating. When moving to another computer, copy those files and select the correct WN-2 identity in your instruments. Audio devices and saved passwords may need to be selected or entered again. A layout file does not contain passwords or device calibration preferences. See  Connection problems  if the program opens but cannot find your WN-2. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "RayWn2App on Windows", "url": "platform-windows.html", "text": "RayWn2App on Windows RayWn2App on Windows \n Start with the installation package supplied with your WN-2 or downloaded from RF StateSide. Keep the instructions supplied with that package: its exact filename and setup prompts may differ between releases. \n Install and open Download the Windows installation package supplied by RF StateSide, or use the package supplied with your WN-2. Close RayWn2App before installing an update. Run the installer and follow its prompts, then leave  Launch RayWn2App  selected on the completion page if you want to open it immediately. The WN-2 USB driver is installed separately. Allow administrator access only for the installer you intended to run. Open RayWn2App from the Start menu or its desktop shortcut. Connect the powered WN-2 with a USB data cable. \n The default installation folder is  C:\\Program Files\\RayWn2App . Its  Layouts  folder contains Single Radio and Radio and Amplifier templates. Use  Layout \u2192 Station Layouts  to choose one and assign your WN-2. Save customized layouts in Documents or another personal folder. Find your WN-2 Press  Discover . Select the entry with your WN-2's serial number, then  Connect . Windows can offer a  D2XX  entry and a  VCP  entry with a COM port. These are two ways to reach the same meter, not two meters. Connect through one at a time. This illustration uses D2XX. Close or disconnect other programs that use the WN-2 before connecting. If Windows changes the COM number after moving the USB cable, discover again and select the matching serial number. \n If the meter does not appear Windows needs the WaveNode USB driver before RayWn2App can discover a physical WN-2.  Download it from  WaveNode Downloads . Choose the USB driver for  Windows 10 and 11  and follow the installation instructions linked on that page. RayWn2App's installer includes its application runtimes but does not install this hardware driver. If Device Manager shows  USB<-->WN-2  under  Other devices  with drivers not installed, install the WaveNode driver, reconnect the cable and press  Discover  again. For a downloaded driver folder, use Device Manager's  Update driver \u2192 Browse my computer for drivers  and include subfolders. Check power and the USB data cable too. The standard and Universal x64 FTDI Windows packages version 2.12.36.20 do not list the WN-2 identifier (0403:B028). Use the WaveNode package for this meter rather than assuming a generic FTDI download will work. Do not change the meter's identity or calibration. If it still does not appear, contact RF StateSide with the Windows version and the message shown in Device Manager. D2XX works, but no VCP COM port appears Windows may require a second driver step for the serial port: Open  Device Manager . If  USB Serial  appears with its driver missing, right-click it and choose  Update driver . Choose  Browse my computer for drivers , select the same extracted  WaveNode driver folder , and enable  Include subfolders . Complete installation. Check  Ports (COM & LPT)  for the new COM port, then press  Discover  in RayWn2App. Select the matching  VCP (COM\u2026)  entry and connect. Disconnect D2XX first; use only one transport for a meter at a time. This second step was verified on Windows 10. D2XX and VCP also passed Windows 11 testing, although that computer may already have had the WaveNode driver installed. COM numbers vary between computers. If Windows cannot find a compatible driver, contact RF StateSide with the exact message rather than forcing an unrelated driver. RayWn2App on Windows, receiving live measurements from physical WN-2 WNVSC7RK with RF off. Select image to enlarge. Check your first readings The status bar should show your meter  Live  and its link healthy. The untouched initial  Untitled layout  automatically assigns all its instruments to the first connected device that sends live measurements. Its bindings are retained for the next session. Saved or edited layouts keep their own assignments. You can also load a supplied station layout and select the WN-2 for it. With the transmitter in receive, near-zero power,  Low RF  and unavailable SWR are normal. They do not mean that the USB connection failed. RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration. Optional  user reference matching  is available if you want readings to match another meter. See  First station connection ,  Device Settings  and  Connection problems . Installation overview  \u00b7  Help home"}, {"title": "RayWn2App on Ubuntu Linux", "url": "platform-ubuntu.html", "text": "RayWn2App on Ubuntu Linux RayWn2App on Ubuntu Linux \n Start with the installation package supplied with your WN-2 or downloaded from RF StateSide. Keep the instructions supplied with that package: its exact filename and setup prompts may differ between releases. \n Install and open Choose the Ubuntu/Linux package for your computer. A typical Intel or AMD desktop uses the x86_64 package; do not use the Raspberry Pi ARM64 package. Extract the complete downloaded archive into a folder. Follow the included installation instructions. For a package containing  install.sh , open a terminal in that folder and run  sh ./install.sh . Let the installation script complete its dependency and WN-2 USB-access setup. It may ask for your administrator password. If it requests a sign-out or restart to activate permissions, complete that step. Open RayWn2App from the application launcher as your normal desktop user. \n Connect by USB Connect and power the WN-2, press  Discover , select its serial number and  VCP , then press  Connect . A serial port may be named  /dev/ttyUSB0 ; that name can change when other USB equipment is connected. Use the meter's serial number to identify it. The illustration shows a physical VCP connection. \n Using D2XX instead D2XX is also supported with the matching driver and USB-access setup. Linux must release this WN-2 from its VCP driver before D2XX can open it. Disconnect in RayWn2App first, then use the WN-2 USB-mode helper supplied with the driver setup, if included. Discover again and select D2XX. Follow the package's instructions to return to VCP. Do not disable drivers for other station equipment. \n Permissions and desktop services If the meter is listed but cannot open, close other programs using it and check that the installation's USB permissions took effect. Do not run the entire application as administrator as a workaround. A desktop keyring prompt can appear when saving an email or MQTT password; unlock it locally. Select your intended playback and microphone devices in the relevant audio settings. RayWn2App on Ubuntu Linux, receiving live measurements from physical WN-2 WNVSC7RK with RF off. Select image to enlarge. Check your first readings The status bar should show your meter  Live  and its link healthy. Load a supplied layout and bind its instruments to that same WN-2. With the transmitter in receive, near-zero power,  Low RF  and unavailable SWR are normal. They do not mean that the USB connection failed. RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration. Optional  user reference matching  is available if you want readings to match another meter. See  First station connection ,  Device Settings  and  Connection problems . Installation overview  \u00b7  Help home"}, {"title": "RayWn2App on Raspberry Pi", "url": "platform-raspberry-pi.html", "text": "RayWn2App on Raspberry Pi RayWn2App on Raspberry Pi \n Start with the installation package supplied with your WN-2 or downloaded from RF StateSide. Keep the instructions supplied with that package: its exact filename and setup prompts may differ between releases. \n Choose the Pi package Use a supported  64-bit Raspberry Pi OS desktop  and the  ARM64  RayWn2App package. The connected illustration was made on a Raspberry Pi 5. An Intel/AMD Linux package or an ARMv7 package is not interchangeable with ARM64. Extract the complete package. Follow its included instructions; if it contains  install.sh , open a terminal in the extracted folder and run  sh ./install.sh . Allow the installer to finish the WN-2 USB-access and desktop dependency setup. Complete any requested sign-out or restart. Start RayWn2App from the desktop application launcher as your normal user. \n Connect the physical WN-2 Use a reliable Pi power supply and a USB data cable. Power the WN-2, press  Discover , select its serial number and VCP, then  Connect . The illustration uses VCP. Its port may appear as  /dev/ttyUSB0 ; the serial number remains the useful device identity. D2XX is available with the ARM64 driver and the WN-2-specific USB setup. As on Ubuntu, disconnect first and use the supplied USB-mode helper, if included, to release VCP before choosing D2XX. Never attempt both connections to the same meter at once. See  Linux D2XX selection . \n Audio, shortcuts and screen size Choose an actual HDMI, USB or other available audio output before using spoken readings, tones or messages.  Dummy Output  means there is no usable playback route; successful installation alone does not make sound available. Recording also needs a microphone. On Wayland desktops, global audio shortcuts may be unavailable; the on-screen message buttons remain available. For a small screen, choose fewer instruments, use the Compact Station Panel or adjust application scaling. Layout files can be shared with other computers; check their WN-2 bindings after loading. Keep adequate free storage for recordings and history exports. RayWn2App on Raspberry Pi, receiving live measurements from physical WN-2 WNVSC7RK with RF off. Select image to enlarge. Check your first readings The status bar should show your meter  Live  and its link healthy. Load a supplied layout and bind its instruments to that same WN-2. With the transmitter in receive, near-zero power,  Low RF  and unavailable SWR are normal. They do not mean that the USB connection failed. RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration. Optional  user reference matching  is available if you want readings to match another meter. See  First station connection ,  Device Settings  and  Connection problems . Installation overview  \u00b7  Help home"}, {"title": "RayWn2App on macOS", "url": "platform-macos.html", "text": "RayWn2App on macOS RayWn2App on macOS \n Start with the installation package supplied with your WN-2 or downloaded from RF StateSide. Keep the instructions supplied with that package: its exact filename and setup prompts may differ between releases. \n Install the application Choose the macOS package matching your Mac. Apple silicon uses ARM64. Use an Intel package only when one is offered for that Mac. Open the downloaded archive or disk image and follow its included instructions to place RayWn2App in  Applications . Complete the WN-2 D2XX driver setup described in that package, then open RayWn2App. If macOS asks you to approve a downloaded component, verify its source before approving it. Do not turn off system security protections. \n Connect with D2XX Connect the powered WN-2 by USB. Press  Discover , choose the matching serial number with  D2XX , then press  Connect . The illustration shows the physical WN-2 connected through D2XX on an Apple silicon Mac. Close other applications using the meter first. Use D2XX for the current macOS setup.  VCP operation with the WN-2's USB identifier is not yet supported by the verified setup. Installing a generic serial driver does not necessarily make this meter appear as a serial port. Do not edit the meter's USB identity or disable macOS security to make VCP appear. \n Using the Mac desktop The application's menus appear in the macOS menu bar at the top of the screen. Saved layouts, instrument properties and Help operate in the same way as on the other platforms. Resize a detached meter to fit your screen, or return it to the dashboard. Allow microphone access if you intend to record audio messages. Secure password storage uses your Mac keychain; complete its unlock or access prompt locally. Audio device names and shortcut availability can differ from Windows, so review the Audio Message Settings after moving a layout. RayWn2App on macOS, receiving live measurements from physical WN-2 WNVSC7RK with RF off. Select image to enlarge. Check your first readings The status bar should show your meter  Live  and its link healthy. Load a supplied layout and bind its instruments to that same WN-2. With the transmitter in receive, near-zero power,  Low RF  and unavailable SWR are normal. They do not mean that the USB connection failed. RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration. Optional  user reference matching  is available if you want readings to match another meter. See  First station connection ,  Device Settings  and  Connection problems . Installation overview  \u00b7  Help home"}, {"title": "D2XX, VCP and multiple WN-2 devices", "url": "connections.html", "text": "\n D2XX, VCP and multiple WN-2 devices \n D2XX, VCP and multiple WN-2 devices \n On this page Choose the correct WN-2 More than one meter Understand the status If no readings appear Choose the correct WN-2 \n The device list identifies each WN-2 by its serial number. D2XX and VCP are two supported ways to connect by USB. VCP entries also show a port, such as COM9 on Windows. Use the connection configured during installation; do not open the same WN-2 through both methods at once. Press Discover after plugging in a device. Select its entry, then Connect. To stop that connection, select the same identity and press Disconnect. \n More than one meter \n Connect each WN-2 separately. Instruments retain their own Device identity and channel, so one layout can show several meters. Switching the toolbar selection does not change those assignments. \n Understand the status \n \n Live / healthy:  new readings are arriving. \n Disconnected:  the selected connection is closed. \n Stale:  recent readings have stopped arriving; old values are not current measurements. \n Device unavailable:  the instrument's assigned identity is not available. \n Low RF:  the link may be healthy, but forward power is too low for a useful SWR reading. \n \n A disconnected source remains clearly identified without rearranging the layout. Select image to enlarge. \n If no readings appear \n Check WN-2 power and the USB cable. Close another program using the meter. Press Discover again. Confirm the instrument's Device identity matches the connected WN-2. If the link was previously working, try Disconnect followed by Connect;  USB Recovery  provides additional options. On Linux, follow the supplied installation script's USB-access instructions if the device is listed but access is denied. Switching between VCP and D2XX may require the supplied connection-mode helper. Do not disconnect unrelated station equipment. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Device Settings: where to start", "url": "device-settings.html", "text": "\n Device Settings: where to start \n Device Settings: where to start \n On this page Open Device Settings Application Connection RF couplers Analog / DC inputs Digital inputs Settings that have their own windows Open Device Settings \n \n Select your WN-2 in the main window's device list. \n Choose  Device \u2192 Settings . \n Check the device identity at the top of the Settings window. This matters when more than one WN-2 is connected. \n Choose the tab you need. Press  OK  to keep changes or  Cancel  to leave the previous settings. Instrument Properties is different: right-click an individual instrument to change its appearance, source or placement. Device Settings applies to the selected WN-2 and its inputs. \n \n Application \n Choose  Theme  and  App Scale Factor . Scale changes are previewed as you move the slider; Cancel returns to the previous size. The normal D2XX setting is  ftd2xx . Leave connection component settings unchanged unless the installation instructions or support staff direct you otherwise. \n Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Application. Theme and scale affect the whole application. Select image to enlarge. \n Connection \n Turn on  Automatically recover this WN-2 connection  if you want the application to keep trying after an unplugged cable or a device power cycle. You still make the first connection with Connect. Disconnect stops recovery. See  Automatic recovery . \n Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Connection. Recovery is optional for each WN-2. Select image to enlarge. \n RF couplers \n Select the coupler model fitted to each channel. RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration for normal use. Leave conversion values at their established values unless you have a specific reason to change them. Use the optional user reference-matching controls only when you want to match a chosen reference meter. This is not required to start using your WN-2. See  Couplers  and  Reference matching . \n Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 RF couplers. Match each channel to its connected coupler. Select image to enlarge. \n Analog / DC inputs \n Set useful labels and units for connected auxiliary sensors and DC measurements. Most operators can leave established conversion settings alone. If an external sensor requires a particular conversion, use its documented value. For an LM35 or LM34 temperature display, add a  Temperature  instrument. Sensor model and \u00b0C/\u00b0F display preference are selected in that instrument's Properties, not here. \n Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Analog / DC inputs. These are shared input settings. Select image to enlarge. \n Digital inputs \n Give each input a meaningful label and select inversion only when required by the connected equipment. Inversion changes how the input state is interpreted on screen. \n Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Digital inputs. Select image to enlarge. \n Settings that have their own windows \n Hardware LCD range, SWR protection and email rules are opened separately from the Device menu. Saving device preferences does not substitute for an Apply to device button in those windows. Read the window's status after applying a hardware setting. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Automatic connection recovery and USB controls", "url": "recovery.html", "text": "\n Automatic connection recovery and USB controls \n Automatic connection recovery and USB controls \n Automatic recovery \n Open  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Connection , select the WN-2 and check  Automatically recover this WN-2 connection . Press OK. Connect normally once; after that, the application can retry if the device loses power or stops responding. \n Recovery is optional and saved separately for each WN-2. Select image to enlarge. \n It keeps waiting for the same device rather than giving up after a fixed period. This is useful when a remote station may power the WN-2 on much later. Selecting Disconnect cancels the retries. Recovery does not switch to another WN-2 or another connection method. When the device returns, wait for fresh readings and a healthy link. Outputs are initialized OFF on reconnection. Capture viewers need Start again; old captures are not new data. \n Explicit USB Recovery \n If a connected WN-2 is behaving unexpectedly, open  Diagnostics \u2192 USB Recovery . Check the selected identity and the action's explanation. Purge receive clears queued incoming data. Reset link is a stronger connection-recovery action where supported. \n Recovery controls show availability and the outcome for the selected WN-2. Select image to enlarge. \n Stop captures and avoid active control operations before recovery. A driver accepting the action is not yet proof that readings have resumed; wait for fresh telemetry. If the problem remains, use Disconnect/Connect and check cable, power and another program's use of the meter. These controls are for connection problems. They do not repair wiring, change calibration or resolve a genuine SWR fault. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Power, SWR and Return Loss", "url": "rf-measurements.html", "text": "\n Power, SWR and Return Loss \n Power, SWR and Return Loss \n On this page Power readings SWR and Return Loss Check the source before comparing Calibration Power readings \n Forward average  is the average power flowing toward the load.  Reflected average  is average power returning from it. The corresponding  peak  readings show higher short-term power levels and are useful with speech or changing signals. Choose the reading that suits your operating activity. A peak-hold display may retain a previous high value; look for Held/Live indications.  Peak hold  explains how to reset it. \n SWR and Return Loss \n SWR indicates the relationship between forward and reflected power. A value close to 1:1 means little reflected power relative to forward power. Return Loss presents related information in dB; a higher return loss means less reflection. When forward power is too low, SWR can be unavailable even though the connection is healthy. Do not interpret an unavailable reading as a perfect match. \n Check the source before comparing \n Each instrument has a Device identity and RF channel. Check its label, channel and coupler selection if the reading is unexpected. Forward and reflected readings come from the existing WN-2 measurements; different average and peak readings need not describe the same instant. \n Calibration \n RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require further calibration for normal use. The selected coupler model must match the connected coupler. Optional user reference matching changes the application's displayed power to agree with a chosen reference; it does not recalibrate the coupler itself. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Couplers, profiles and hardware LCD ranges", "url": "couplers.html", "text": "\n Couplers, profiles and hardware LCD ranges \n Couplers, profiles and hardware LCD ranges \n Select the installed coupler \n Open  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 RF couplers . Check the WN-2 identity and choose the model installed on each channel. Press OK to save your choices. Do this before judging power readings or comparing amplifier input and output. \n Coupler choices are kept separately for each WN-2 and channel. Select image to enlarge. \n RF couplers are factory calibrated and do not require additional calibration. For normal operation, select the correct model and use its established settings. Do not change conversion values simply because a display scale looks wrong. \n Display range is a different setting \n An instrument's Maximum controls its on-screen scale. It does not change the coupler's rating or the WN-2 LCD range. Automatic range changes only the application display. Choose  Device \u2192 LCD Display  to change the WN-2's own LCD full-scale range. Select the intended device, enter the supported range, then Apply. Read the status and check the physical display. \n The hardware LCD setting is separate from dashboard meter ranges. Select image to enlarge. \n Optional reference matching \n If you want RayWn2App to agree with another meter, use  User reference matching . You may use a reference that suits your station, from a laboratory meter to the wattmeter in an amplifier. It is an optional comparison, not a requirement to operate the WN-2. Settings are retained by device, channel and coupler model. When changing a coupler model, review the selected settings rather than assuming one model's reference curve applies to another. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "User reference-matching calibration", "url": "reference-calibration.html", "text": "\n User reference-matching calibration \n User reference-matching calibration \n On this page When to use this feature Open the editor Build a curve What changes Review or return to normal When to use this feature \n Your RF couplers are factory calibrated; additional calibration is not required. User reference matching is optional. Use it when you want RayWn2App's power readings to follow a particular meter of your choosing. The reference may be a laboratory instrument or the wattmeter built into your amplifier. You do not have to supply a laboratory certificate or a pre-existing reference data file. The result follows the reference you choose, including its limitations. \n Open the editor \n Open  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 RF couplers , select the intended WN-2/channel/model and click  User reference calibration . Check the identity and coupler before entering points. \n Build a curve \n \n Begin with the existing normal coupler settings. \n Establish a stable power level that is appropriate for your station and load. \n Select Forward or Reflected in the editor.  Capture ADC  freezes the selected input's raw reading. Enter the corresponding watts shown by your reference meter. \n Click  Add point . Repeat at useful levels across your operating range, up to 20 points total shared between forward and reflected for that device/channel/model. Match each direction to a reference measurement of that same direction. \n Review the points and check  Use user reference matching , press OK, then press OK in Settings when satisfied. Use increasing, sensible pairs and avoid duplicate or contradictory points. Do not use clipped or invalid readings. You control the transmitter; the editor does not generate RF or set drive power. \n \n What changes \n The application adjusts displayed power between the entered points. Each curve belongs to one WN-2, channel and coupler model. It is not a general correction for every coupler or device. Related application calculations use the corrected readings. This does not alter the factory calibration inside the coupler or the WN-2's own LCD/protection behavior. Keep that distinction in mind if the application and hardware displays differ. \n Review or return to normal \n Keep a record of the reference and conditions used. If the reference or station changes, review the curve. Disabling user reference matching returns to the underlying coupler conversion; it does not require deleting your saved points. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "DC and analog inputs", "url": "auxiliary.html", "text": "\n DC and analog inputs \n DC and analog inputs \n On this page Add an auxiliary reading Set labels and units Bar displays Temperature Add an auxiliary reading \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Add instrument . Select your WN-2, then  DC voltage ,  DC current  or  Analog input  as Measurement. Analog input also needs an input number. Choose a digital reading or a supported horizontal/vertical bar and give it a useful label. \n Set labels and units \n Open  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Analog / DC inputs . The input label, units and conversion belong to the selected device. Use the settings appropriate to the connected sensor or current shunt. \n Analog and DC input setup is separate from instrument placement and appearance. Select image to enlarge. \n Do not assume an unconnected input represents a real sensor. For current measurement, the required sensing connection must be installed; powering the WN-2 alone does not measure your station's current. \n Bar displays \n Set a maximum and threshold colors in Instrument Properties. Bars start with a two-row footprint. Resize them in Edit Layout to suit your screen. Colors are display aids, not hardware switching thresholds. \n Temperature \n For LM35/LM34 sensors, choose  Temperature  rather than a generic Analog input instrument. The  Temperature topic  explains model, units and wiring. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "LM35 and LM34 temperature instruments", "url": "temperature.html", "text": "\n LM35 and LM34 temperature instruments \n LM35 and LM34 temperature instruments \n On this page Add a temperature instrument Connect the sensor Check the reading Range and warnings Add a temperature instrument \n \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Add instrument . \n Select the WN-2 and set  Measurement  to  Temperature . \n Select the auxiliary input connected to the sensor. \n Choose  Sensor model: LM35 or LM34 . \n Choose  Display units: Celsius or Fahrenheit . Either sensor model can display either unit. \n Choose Digital, Horizontal or Vertical, enter a label such as Heatsink, then place the instrument and press OK. \n \n Temperature model and display units belong to the instrument's Properties. Select image to enlarge. \n You can show the same sensor in \u00b0C and \u00b0F using two instruments. Changing one instrument does not change the other's preference. \n Connect the sensor \n For the WN-2's 16-pin rear expansion connector, analog inputs 1\u20134 are pins 10, 11, 12 and 13 respectively. The +5 V connection is pin 16; ground is pin 9 or pin 14. Pin 15 is spare, not ground. Confirm the connector orientation and pin-1 marking before wiring; looking from the wire side reverses the view. Connect the sensor's supply, ground and output according to the exact sensor package's pinout. The output goes to the chosen analog input. Do not assume every LM35/LM34 package has the same lead arrangement. Do not apply these pin numbers to a different WaveNode model or connector. Use suitable wiring and decoupling for an RF environment. Check the temperature against a known thermometer when first installing the sensor. A disconnected or shorted sensor can sometimes produce a plausible reading; the application cannot identify every wiring fault. \n Check the reading \n LM35 produces 0.01 V per \u00b0C; LM34 produces 0.01 V per \u00b0F. For example, an LM34 output of 0.77 V corresponds to 77 \u00b0F, which is 25 \u00b0C. Choose the actual sensor model even when displaying the other unit. An Analog input instrument can show the input voltage beside the temperature. Extra decimal places improve readability but do not increase sensor accuracy. A simple single-supply sensor connection does not automatically measure the sensor's full negative-temperature range. \n Range and warnings \n Set bar limits and color thresholds in the selected display units. Review them after switching \u00b0C/\u00b0F. Invalid, stale or disconnected readings are identified instead of being presented as a trustworthy temperature. Email threshold rules use the device's configured analog value/units, which may differ from an individual Temperature instrument; check those separately. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Digital inputs and latched or momentary outputs", "url": "digital-io.html", "text": "\n Digital inputs and latched or momentary outputs \n Digital inputs and latched or momentary outputs \n Inputs \n Add a  Digital input  instrument, select its WN-2 and input number, and give it a descriptive label. Open  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Digital inputs  to set device-level labels and inversion where needed. \n Label inputs according to the equipment actually connected. Select image to enlarge. \n Outputs \n Add a  Digital output  instrument and select the intended output. In Properties choose  Latched  or  Momentary . Latched operation keeps the requested state until changed. Momentary operation turns the output on for the selected duration, then requests OFF. Check the configured duration before using it with external equipment. Return to normal layout mode before operating output controls. They are not active while arranging the layout. \n External equipment \n WN-2 logic outputs need the appropriate external interface. Do not drive relay coils directly. Label controls so it is clear what they operate. A displayed command state is not proof that the external relay or device moved. Connection/reconnection initializes the digital outputs OFF. A communications failure can prevent a later command from reaching the equipment. Do not rely on a software-timed pulse as the only safeguard for equipment that must return to a safe state. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Layouts, groups and templates", "url": "layouts.html", "text": "\n Layouts, groups and templates \n Layouts, groups and templates \n On this page What is a layout? Start with an example Other arrangements you can create Groups keep related instruments together Save your work Make your first changes What is a layout? \n A layout is the arrangement of meters on your screen. Think of it as a customizable instrument panel for your station. You decide what to show, how large each meter should be, where it belongs and which WN-2 channel it reads. You are not limited to the supplied arrangements. Combine digital readouts, horizontal or vertical bars, analog meters, cross-needle meters, gain, temperature and other instruments. Make a compact panel beside your logging software or a larger monitoring screen. Save as many named layout files as you need for your operating activities. \n Start with an example \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Station Layouts . Select  Single Radio \u2014 HF/VHF  for one coupler, or  Radio and Amplifier \u2014 HF/VHF  for input/output monitoring. Select your WN-2 identity when prompted and click Load Layout. \n Single-radio example: a power bar with useful digital readings below it. Select image to enlarge. \n Radio-and-amplifier example: separate input and output groups plus amplifier gain. Select image to enlarge. \n These are starting points, not fixed screens. You can change any instrument, add more, remove what you do not need, and save your own version. Configure the actual coupler models separately in Device Settings. \n Other arrangements you can create \n A cross-needle station display. Choose Combined RF Channel with Analog display to create this style. Select image to enlarge. \n A compact horizontal Combined RF Channel meter gathers related readings in one instrument. Select image to enlarge. \n The same measurements arranged vertically for a different available screen space. Select image to enlarge. \n For a contest, keep only the essential power and SWR readings visible. For amplifier setup, include input/output power, gain and DC readings. For station monitoring, add temperatures and digital inputs. Multiple instruments can show the same channel in different styles, and one layout can include more than one WN-2. \n Groups keep related instruments together \n A group is a named section such as Radio, Amplifier or Antenna. Each group has its own rows and columns. Instruments can occupy one cell or span several cells. The dashboard also has a separate setting for how many groups appear across the screen. Use  Layout \u2192 Group columns  to change groups across. Use an instrument's Properties in Edit Layout to change columns inside its group. These are different settings. \n Save your work \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Save Layout As  and give the arrangement a clear name. Save updates that file; Save As makes another named version. Open Layout loads a saved arrangement. An asterisk in the title bar means something has changed since the last save. The application remembers your layout when you close normally. Layouts save instrument choices, positions, labels and device assignments. They do not save passwords, running recordings or held peak history. \n Make your first changes \n Start with a copy of a supplied layout. Follow  Layout Edit Mode  to move, resize and duplicate instruments. You can Undo an edit, so there is no need to rebuild the whole dashboard after an unwanted move. To use a saved layout with another WN-2, change its instrument Device identity values. Renaming a group does not change those assignments. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Layout Edit Mode: arranging your station", "url": "layout-mode.html", "text": "\n Layout Edit Mode: arranging your station \n Layout Edit Mode: arranging your station \n On this page Before you begin 1. Enter Edit Layout 2. Understand the grid 3. Add an instrument 4. Move a meter 5. Resize a meter 6. Undo and Redo 7. Use a finer grid 8. Copy an instrument or an entire group 9. Return to normal operation Common questions Before you begin \n Open a layout close to the arrangement you want. Choose  Layout \u2192 Save Layout As  and give your practice copy a new name. Keep the original if you want an easy way back. The examples below use a connected WN-2 with RF idle. You can arrange a layout without transmitting. The instrument positions remain the same when readings change. \n 1. Enter Edit Layout \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Edit Layout , or press Ctrl+E. Movement and resize handles appear around instruments. This mode is for arranging the display; return to normal mode when you want to operate instrument controls. \n Edit Layout exposes handles and grid guides. Select image to enlarge. \n 2. Understand the grid \n Right-click an instrument and choose  Properties . In Edit Layout, the window includes Group, Columns in this group, Row, Column, Row span and Column span. Row 1 and Column 1 are the upper-left position inside the group. A span tells you how many cells the instrument occupies. For example, Column span 2 stretches an instrument across two columns. Row span 2 makes it occupy two rows. \n Geometry controls appear in Properties while Edit Layout is enabled. Select image to enlarge. \n A blank-looking area may still be part of a tall instrument. Two instruments cannot occupy the same cell. Hover over the grid or inspect Properties when a position appears unavailable. \n 3. Add an instrument \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Add instrument , or right-click an empty grid position. Select  Device identity , then the required  Measurement  and its channel or input. Choose a Display style and a useful Label. Select the destination Group, position and spans.  Find first free position  proposes a location that does not overlap another instrument. Check it, then press OK. For a new group, type a new group name in the Group field. Choose its number of columns. You can rename the group later. See  Adding a cross-needle meter  for a complete example and  Instrument choices  for other styles. \n 4. Move a meter \n Drag its movement handle to a free position, then release. For an exact position, change Row and Column in Properties. Press Escape to cancel a move that is still in progress. \n Moving a meter can deliberately leave space for another instrument. Select image to enlarge. \n If moving into another group, check that group's available cells and grid spacing. A different group name does not select a different WN-2; change Device identity separately if needed. \n 5. Resize a meter \n Use the edge or corner resize handles to change its width, height or both. You can also enter Row span and Column span in Properties. Increase the width for long labels or more readable scales; increase the height for vertical bars and analog faces. Meters keep a minimum readable size. Another instrument sharing a row can also affect that row's height. A grid span is a layout allocation, not a fixed number of screen pixels. \n 6. Undo and Redo \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Undo  after an unwanted move, size change or property edit. Redo restores the edit. These commands apply to the layout, not to relay actions or recorded measurements. \n Undo returns to the preceding arrangement. Select image to enlarge. \n 7. Use a finer grid \n Right-click the group heading and select  Refine Group Grid . Check Vertical, Horizontal or both to divide existing grid tracks into smaller parts. Examine the preview before pressing OK. \n The preview explains which areas are occupied and how the grid will change. Select image to enlarge. \n Refinement preserves the space already occupied by each instrument. It does not automatically create an empty cell. After refining, shrink an instrument by one of the smaller tracks to make room for another. \n A refined group allows more precise arrangements. Select image to enlarge. \n Each direction can be refined once. If a small instrument will not fit when moved to a different group, compare their grid refinement settings. Undo can reverse refinement. \n 8. Copy an instrument or an entire group \n Right-click an instrument and choose Duplicate to make another with the same properties. Place the copy, then change its label, channel or device as required. For a matching station section, use  Layout \u2192 Duplicate Group , or right-click the group heading and choose Duplicate group. Give the copy a unique name. This preserves the original group's sizes, positions and grid spacing, so you do not have to reconstruct it instrument by instrument. \n Duplicate Group preserves the arrangement. Reassign copied instruments if they should read another WN-2. Select image to enlarge. \n The copied meters initially read the same sources. Open their Properties to change Device identity or channel. Connect another WN-2 separately before expecting its readings. One Undo removes the group copy. \n 9. Return to normal operation \n Turn  Edit Layout  off. The handles disappear. Right-click Properties remains available for ordinary source and appearance settings, but the geometry controls are hidden to prevent accidental rearrangement. \n Normal-mode Properties keeps measurement and display choices available. Select image to enlarge. \n Check the readings and labels, save the layout, and reopen it if you want to confirm the saved arrangement. \n Common questions \n \n Why will a meter not fit in apparently empty space?  A spanning instrument may already own those cells. Inspect its row and column spans. \n Why do two groups look different?  Compare columns, row/column spans and refinement. Duplicate Group is the easiest way to start with identical arrangements. \n Why is a copied meter disconnected?  Its Device identity may still refer to the original WN-2. A group name does not establish a connection. \n Where did my meter go?  Check View visibility options and any detached meter windows. \n Can I make a layout for another activity?  Yes. Save As under a new name and customize that copy. \n \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Instrument selection and properties", "url": "instruments.html", "text": "\n Instrument selection and properties \n Instrument selection and properties \n On this page Choose a measurement, then its appearance Useful choices Change an existing instrument Make one meter easier to see Choose a measurement, then its appearance \n An instrument is one meter in your layout. Its Measurement chooses what it reads; Display chooses how it looks. You can show the same measurement more than once, for example a large power bar beside a small numeric reading. Open  Layout \u2192 Add instrument . Choose Device identity and channel/input first, then Measurement, Display and Label. Set a suitable maximum for a scale. Choose its group and placement, and press OK. \n Useful choices \n \n Your purpose Measurement Suggested display \n Watch transmitter output Forward average or Forward peak Horizontal, Vertical, Digital or Analog \n Watch reflected power Reflected average or Reflected peak Digital or bar \n See related RF readings together Combined RF Channel Horizontal or Vertical \n Read a traditional cross-needle face Combined RF Channel Analog \n Check antenna match SWR or Return Loss Digital \n Compare amplifier input/output Amplifier Gain Digital \n Monitor supply or sensors DC voltage, DC current, Analog input, Temperature Digital or supported bar display \n Observe or operate external connections Digital input or Digital output Digital control/readout \n Keep a small waveform in the layout Mini-Scope Its waveform display \n \n A Combined RF Channel meter keeps average/peak, forward/reflected and SWR readings together. Select image to enlarge. \n Change an existing instrument \n Right-click it and choose Properties. In normal operation, you can change measurement and appearance without moving it. Turn Edit Layout on when you want to change group, position or size. Give meters clear labels. Set range and threshold colors to suit your station, remembering that display colors do not set protection thresholds. See  Meter ranges and peak hold . \n Make one meter easier to see \n Right-click and choose  Enlarge in separate window . Resize that window to suit the display. Close it or choose Return to dashboard to put it back. See  Floating displays . \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Analog and cross-needle meters", "url": "analog-meters.html", "text": "\n Analog and cross-needle meters \n Analog and cross-needle meters \n On this page Add a cross-needle meter Add a single-needle power meter Colors and scale Improve readability Add a cross-needle meter \n The cross-needle meter shows forward power on one scale and reflected power on the other. Where the needles cross, read SWR against the curved guide lines. \n \n Choose  Layout \u2192 Add instrument . \n Select your WN-2 in  Device identity , then the RF channel you want to watch. \n Set  Measurement  to  Combined RF Channel . \n Set  Display  to  Analog . This combination selects the cross-needle face. \n Enter a label such as Station power and SWR and choose an appropriate power maximum. \n Choose a group and enough space for the face. Press  OK , then resize in Edit Layout if needed. \n \n Add instrument with Combined RF Channel and Analog selected. Select image to enlarge. \n A dedicated cross-needle layout. With RF idle, Low RF explains why SWR is unavailable. Select image to enlarge. \n The forward and reflected needles use average power. The numeric readings below the face include the related peak readings. At very low power there may not be enough information for a useful SWR ratio. Read the status as well as the needle positions. \n Add a single-needle power meter \n Choose  Forward average ,  Forward peak ,  Reflected average  or  Reflected peak  as Measurement and  Analog  as Display. This gives one power scale and a large numeric value. \n A detached single-needle meter sized to keep the dial prominent. Select image to enlarge. \n Colors and scale \n In Properties, choose the scale's Low, Middle and High colors and where the color changes occur. Percentage thresholds follow the displayed range; absolute thresholds remain at the watt values you enter. Needle and peak-marker colors can also be changed. The color band is thicker from zero up to the displayed needle value, making the reading easier to see at a glance. The rest of the scale stays thin. The peak marker is separate from the needle; turning its visibility off does not disable peak hold. The color zones are visual aids. They do not set the WN-2 SWR trip point or change a coupler's rated power. \n Improve readability \n Use a larger footprint for an analog face than for a digital number. Short labels leave more space for the scales. Right-click  Enlarge in separate window  for a closer view without rebuilding the dashboard. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Auto range, peak hold and markers", "url": "meter-dynamics.html", "text": "\n Auto range, peak hold and markers \n Auto range, peak hold and markers \n On this page Set a useful display range Peak Hold Reset a held reading Peak marker and colors Set a useful display range \n Right-click a meter and choose  Properties . Maximum sets its scale. Choose a range that makes ordinary readings easy to see without repeatedly reaching the top. Where available,  Automatic range  lets the display expand for larger readings and settle back to a smaller range. It does not change the coupler rating, factory calibration, hardware LCD or SWR protection limit. \n Peak Hold \n Applicable peak instruments and Combined RF Channel meters offer three modes: \n \n Off:  follow the current peak reading. \n Timed:  retain a peak for the selected time, then allow it to fall. \n Until Reset:  retain the highest observed peak until you reset it. Timed hold can be adjusted in 250 ms steps. A short setting around 500 ms is a useful starting point for speech; choose what is comfortable for your operation. Existing saved choices remain in effect. \n \n Reset a held reading \n Use the instrument's  Reset display hold/range  action. Forward and reflected holds remain independent where both are shown. Held is a previous high value, not a fresh measurement. Invalid or lost-source conditions remain visible rather than being hidden by the retained peak. The hold preference is saved in the layout. Captured peak history is not restored after restarting the application. \n Peak marker and colors \n Show peak marker  controls the thin marker on a bar or basic analog meter. Turning it off does not turn off peak tracking or change the held numeric value. Set Low/Middle/High colors and thresholds in Properties. Percentage thresholds move with the scale; absolute thresholds stay at the entered values. These are visual zones, not alarm settings. \n Meter behavior is configured in ordinary Properties; Edit Layout is needed only for geometry. Select image to enlarge. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Compact Station Panel and enlarged meters", "url": "floating-panels.html", "text": "\n Compact Station Panel and enlarged meters \n Compact Station Panel and enlarged meters \n Compact Station Panel \n Choose  Tools \u2192 Compact Station Panel . Select the WN-2, check the channels you want to show, and choose Horizontal or Vertical. Set Columns to arrange the selected channels across the panel. Use it beside your logging or radio-control software. On top keeps it above other windows. Hide main reduces the space occupied by the main dashboard; use the panel's return/show control to bring it back. The panel remembers its size and position when reopened. \n Compact Station Panel connected to the selected WN-2. Select image to enlarge. \n Enlarge one instrument \n Right-click a dashboard meter and choose  Enlarge in separate window . Drag the window border to make the instrument easy to read. A horizontal bar normally needs a wide, shallow window; an analog meter benefits from more height. \n A wide, shallow detached bar display, without unused space below it. Select image to enlarge. \n An analog meter enlarged to make the face prominent. Select image to enlarge. \n Click  Return to dashboard , or close the detached window, to put the instrument back. Its original dashboard space is kept for it. It remains the same instrument with the same source and settings. \n Which should I use? \n Use the Compact Station Panel for several channels in a separate small window. Use detached enlargement when one particular meter needs a larger view. Neither requires a separate WN-2 connection. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Themes, scale, colors and visibility", "url": "appearance.html", "text": "\n Themes, scale, colors and visibility \n Themes, scale, colors and visibility \n On this page Choose a theme Change the size of controls Hide readings you do not currently need Instrument colors Choose a theme \n Open the  Theme  menu and select the appearance you prefer. Try a dark theme for a dim shack or a light theme for a bright room. Readings, saved positions and device assignments remain the same. \n Change the size of controls \n Open  Device \u2192 Settings \u2192 Application . Move  App Scale Factor  to make text and controls larger or smaller. The change is previewed immediately. OK keeps it; Cancel returns to the previous size. Reset to 1.00 returns to the normal application size. \n Application settings with the normal ftd2xx connection setting. Select image to enlarge. \n You can also resize the main window. If a layout is crowded, reduce the number of groups across, choose shorter labels or enlarge selected instruments. Help has its own A\u2212/A+ reading-size buttons. \n Hide readings you do not currently need \n Use  View \u2192 Show Amplifier Gain instruments  or  Show DC voltage/current instruments . These switches hide the matching instruments without deleting them. Turn them back on to restore the display. \n Instrument colors \n Use Instrument Properties for bar/analog scale colors, thresholds and peak-marker visibility. Use Device Settings for channel identification colors. Display colors are separate from alarms and protection limits. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Scope, Mini-Scope and capture lifecycle", "url": "scope.html", "text": "\n Scope, Mini-Scope and capture lifecycle \n Scope, Mini-Scope and capture lifecycle \n On this page Open and start Scope Stop, Pause and Resume Trigger and Settings Inspect the waveform Mini-Scope Open and start Scope \n Choose  Capture \u2192 Scope . Select your WN-2 and the required Scope Sensor, then press  Start . Wait for a waveform to arrive. Scope shows a captured RF envelope, not the radio-frequency carrier itself. \n Scope demonstration using a simulated 500 Hz envelope from the WN-2 emulator; no RF transmission. Select image to enlarge. \n Stop, Pause and Resume \n Pause  freezes the displayed waveform so you can inspect it. It does not mean the transmitter stopped.  Resume  returns to updates.  Stop  releases this viewer's capture request. If another view still uses the same device capture, it can remain active.  Capture \u2192 Stop selected device capture  stops capture for the WN-2 selected in the main toolbar. \n Trigger and Settings \n Trigger settings decide when a waveform is held. Auto is a convenient starting point. Other trigger choices let you wait for a chosen waveform condition; use Arm when the selected mode requires it. The trigger reference line helps show the selected level. Open Settings to choose time/voltage presentation, digital scale and vertical range. Start with Auto Scale Y to fit the waveform. Read  Time, voltage and clipping  before changing digital scale to diagnose flattened peaks. \n Inspect the waveform \n Use the Zoom controls for a closer view. Reset Zoom returns to the broader view. Paused or stopped captures remain historical; check the status before treating a displayed trace as current RF. Title changes the plot title. Export PNG saves the visible plot for your records. See  Plot titles and images . \n Mini-Scope \n Add  Mini-Scope  to a layout when you want a small waveform beside the meters. Select its device/sensor in Properties and operate its capture controls in normal layout mode. The main Scope provides a larger view and more analysis controls. \n Practice without transmitting Open the  WN-2 Raw Data Emulator , connect its identity in the main device list, and enable its Scope source. Set the envelope frequency to 500 Hz. Select that same emulator in this viewer and press Start. The illustration uses the nominal 40 ksample/s model. Simulated captures are identified on the axes and status line. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Scope timebase, voltage, Vpp and clipping", "url": "scope-units.html", "text": "\n Scope timebase, voltage, Vpp and clipping \n Scope timebase, voltage, Vpp and clipping \n On this page What the axes mean Auto Scale Y Flattened peaks and clipping Timing limits What the axes mean \n Time is shown using the WN-2's nominal 40,000 samples per second. It is a useful operating timebase, not a precision time measurement. Changing the digital range changes the displayed amplitude interpretation, not the sampling rate. Voltage is the nominal voltage at the measurement input. It is not the RF voltage on your coaxial cable. Raw counts and sample-number views are available for detailed investigation, but are not needed for ordinary waveform viewing. \n Auto Scale Y \n Auto Scale Y fits the waveform vertically and is enabled for new Scope settings. The view expands promptly when a larger waveform arrives and waits before shrinking, which helps keep the display steady. If you saved manual limits, those choices are retained. Vertical zoom changes to manual scaling. Use Settings to return to Auto Scale Y. Auto scaling changes the view; it cannot recover a clipped waveform. \n Flattened peaks and clipping \n If broad peaks look flat, check the digital scale and the clipping message. The high-power scale can accommodate a larger signal than the more sensitive scale. Use the setting appropriate to the signal; do not assume changing the scale changes transmitter power. Captured Vpp is the highest minus lowest voltage in the capture. When clipping or invalid data prevents a trustworthy result, the display explains why Vpp is unavailable. Do not use an unavailable Vpp value as a valid measurement. \n Simulated envelope example: axis units and the status below the plot identify the emulator and explain how to read the capture. Select image to enlarge. \n Timing limits \n The nominal timebase is the same for the physical digital ranges. A previously observed waveform-period discrepancy has not been fully resolved, so avoid treating the time or frequency axes as precision-calibrated measurements. The emulator's optional synthetic rate is explained separately in its own topic. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Envelope Spectrum and modulation bands", "url": "spectrum.html", "text": "\n Envelope Spectrum and modulation bands \n Envelope Spectrum and modulation bands \n On this page View the envelope spectrum Make a useful comparison Band readings Pause and save View the envelope spectrum \n Choose  Capture \u2192 Envelope Spectrum . Select the WN-2 and sensor, then Start. The graph shows frequency components of the captured envelope. It is not a spectrum analyzer for the RF carrier frequency. \n Envelope Spectrum of a simulated 500 Hz envelope. The nearest peak bin is about 508 Hz; this is not the RF carrier. Select image to enlarge. \n Make a useful comparison \n Choose the desired window and averaging options, then allow new captures to arrive. These choices affect how the trace is presented. Keep them consistent when comparing two operating conditions. Read the frequency and magnitude units on the axes. Peaks identify stronger components in the displayed envelope. Frequency spacing is limited by the capture length; zooming makes the graph larger but does not add measurement resolution. \n Band readings \n Where band controls are shown, set the frequency ranges of interest and read the corresponding band results. Use them to compare portions of the envelope spectrum under consistent settings. They are not direct measurements of RF emissions outside the transmitter's band. \n Pause and save \n Pause holds the displayed result for inspection. Reset Zoom returns to the wider view. Use Title for a meaningful plot name and Export PNG for an image. Check source status and Scope clipping before drawing conclusions from an old or distorted trace. The Tone Generator can coordinate a limited capture sequence with an audio stimulus; see  Tone Generator . \n Practice without transmitting Open the  WN-2 Raw Data Emulator , connect its identity in the main device list, and enable its Scope source. Set the envelope frequency to 500 Hz. Select that same emulator in this viewer and press Start. The illustration uses the nominal 40 ksample/s model. Simulated captures are identified on the axes and status line. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Editable plot titles and PNG exports", "url": "plot-exports.html", "text": "\n Editable plot titles and PNG exports \n Editable plot titles and PNG exports \n On this page Give the plot a useful title Export PNG Keep numbers as well as pictures If saving fails Give the plot a useful title \n Use  Title  in the plot window and enter a name that describes the measurement, such as 20 m antenna check or Amplifier envelope. Keeping the existing title is also fine. The title is a label for your record; it does not change the source or measurement. \n Export PNG \n \n Arrange the plot, zoom and scale as you want them recorded. \n Pause the view if you need a fixed captured result. \n Click  Export PNG . \n Choose a folder and filename, then Save. The image contains the plot rather than unrelated application controls. Scope, Envelope Spectrum and Plot SWR use the RF StateSide logo in a reserved footer. Axes, units, traces and plot context remain visible. \n \n Keep numbers as well as pictures \n PNG is useful for sharing and station notes. For later numerical analysis, use the viewer's CSV or saved-data option where available. A picture does not contain the complete measurement history needed to resume a recording or sweep. \n If saving fails \n Choose a folder you can write to and check available disk space. Cancel leaves your plot unchanged. Review the filename before replacing an existing image. A paused or disconnected plot may contain an older capture, so include an appropriate description in your records. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "RF History and Amplifier Analysis", "url": "rf-history.html", "text": "\n RF History and Amplifier Analysis \n RF History and Amplifier Analysis \n On this page Start a history view Read the displays Station events Stop and save Start a history view \n Open  Tools \u2192 RF History and Amplifier Analysis . Select the WN-2 and channels you want to monitor. Choose the readings and interval, then click  Start monitoring . \n RF History combines plots, recording controls, totals and station events. Select image to enlarge. \n The graph records how readings change over time. It is useful for checking a transmission, watching supply conditions or comparing amplifier input and output. Gaps mean valid data was not available; they are not automatically filled with zero. \n Read the displays \n Select the available RF/DC history or amplifier-analysis view for your task. A histogram groups readings to show how often values occur; it is different from the time plot. Gain/linearity views help compare input and output conditions without changing transmitter drive. \n Station events \n The event list records relevant changes alongside measurements, such as connection loss, protection trips and recovery. Use the timestamps to compare a warning with the surrounding readings. Events observed by the application are not proof of every physical event at the station. \n Stop and save \n Stop monitoring  ends acquisition while retaining the displayed results. Save or export the records you need before clearing them. For timed operation, CSV writing, energy and charge totals, follow  Recording and totals . Choose a meaningful title before exporting a graph. If you change device, channel or calibration context, start a distinct record rather than treating incompatible readings as one continuous test. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "System and Module Gain instruments", "url": "amplifier-gain.html", "text": "\n System and Module Gain instruments \n System and Module Gain instruments \n On this page Connect the measurements Add or configure Gain System and module gain Watch changes over time Connect the measurements \n Use one RF coupler before the amplifier and another after it. In the supplied amplifier layout, CH1 is the input and CH2 the output. Select the actual models in Device Settings. \n Add or configure Gain \n Choose  Amplifier Gain  as the instrument Measurement. Select the WN-2, Amp In RF Channel and Amp Out RF Channel. Use different channels with the correct physical roles. Give the instrument a useful label. Choose  Gain units  as dB or  Power ratio (\u00d7) . For example, an output ten times the input is 10\u00d7, or 10 dB. Switching units changes the presentation, not the underlying measurements. \n System and module gain \n System Gain compares the measured output with the measured input. If using Module Gain with an input attenuator allowance, enter the appropriate attenuation in Properties. Do not add an allowance unless it represents your actual arrangement. Low RF, disconnected or invalid inputs prevent a useful gain calculation. The readings are not guaranteed to describe precisely the same instant, so compare stable operating conditions where practical. \n Watch changes over time \n Use  Tools \u2192 RF History and Amplifier Analysis  to compare input/output trends. The application does not adjust transmitter drive. You remain responsible for the station's operating limits. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Timed recording, RF/DC energy and charge", "url": "recording.html", "text": "\n Timed recording, RF/DC energy and charge \n Timed recording, RF/DC energy and charge \n On this page Set up a recording Timed or continuous operation Energy and charge Reset and changed conditions File problems Practical long recordings Set up a recording \n Open  Tools \u2192 RF History and Amplifier Analysis  and select the WN-2 and channels. Choose the measurements and recording interval. Decide whether to record for a set duration or continue until you stop it. Click  Start CSV log  and choose a destination to write a live CSV file.  Export CSV  saves the readings retained in the window. CSV can be opened in a spreadsheet program. Keep the recording window's status visible during a long run so you notice storage problems. \n Timed or continuous operation \n Click  Start monitoring  and check that elapsed time and sample counts increase. A timed run completes when its configured duration ends. Continuous operation continues until you stop it. Stopping retains the results for review and export. \n Check elapsed time, data coverage and writer status during a recording. Select image to enlarge. \n Energy and charge \n RF energy is accumulated in Wh. DC energy is also in Wh, and DC charge is in Ah. These totals depend on valid measurements over time, not just the last displayed value. Only valid intervals are included. Disconnection, stale readings and other gaps reduce coverage; the application does not assume the last power continued throughout a missing interval. Review valid-data coverage and gap information with every total. \n Reset and changed conditions \n Save useful results before  Reset / Clear . Begin a separate record when changing the device, channels or measurement settings that affect interpretation. Do not combine two different station conditions merely because their numeric values look similar. \n File problems \n A full disk, removed drive or unwritable folder can interrupt recording. Read the error and choose a usable destination. Do not assume a graph still updating means every point is reaching the file. Export retained results where possible and note any gap. \n Practical long recordings \n Use a reliable local destination, check available space, and prevent unwanted computer sleep. Keep the WN-2 powered. After the run, review coverage and completion status before relying on totals. Station-event entries can help explain missing intervals. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Manual SWR sweeps", "url": "manual-sweep.html", "text": "\n Manual SWR sweeps \n Manual SWR sweeps \n Prepare a sweep \n Open  Tools \u2192 Plot SWR  and select  Manual sweep . Choose your WN-2 and the required channels in the CH menu. Expand Settings to enter start/end frequency and either a step size or a point count. Choose a range suitable for your station and antenna. Allow room inside band limits for the transmitted signal. The application does not key the transmitter or tune the radio in Manual mode. \n Plot SWR ready for a manual run. Select image to enlarge. \n Capture points \n \n Tune the radio to the displayed  Tune radio to  frequency. \n Establish the intended stable RF condition. \n Click  Capture point  and wait for the result. \n After a successful point, tune to the next requested frequency and repeat. If a point cannot be captured, the requested frequency stays for retry. Previous/Next changes the proposed frequency; it does not change an existing record. Skip point deliberately advances without a reading. Stop retains results. \n \n Finish and save \n The plan is complete when every point has been captured or skipped. Save the sweep for reopening, Export CSV for numbers, or Export PNG for an image. Clear / Restart starts over after checking for unsaved results. With several channels selected, all need valid readings to complete a manual point. See  Multi-channel comparisons . \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Rig-assisted sweeps and Hamlib setup", "url": "rig-sweep.html", "text": "\n Rig-assisted sweeps and Hamlib setup \n Rig-assisted sweeps and Hamlib setup \n On this page Choose rig-assisted operation Set up a managed Hamlib connection Plan and run Stop and restoration Choose rig-assisted operation \n Open  Tools \u2192 Plot SWR , choose Rig-assisted sweep, then open  Hamlib Setup . Choose the connection appropriate to your radio: managed Hamlib, an existing rigctld connection, or native PowerSDR/Thetis CAT. Select the radio/model, connection and speed required by your station. Use Test Rig to check the frequency readback. An open connection alone does not prove the radio is responding. For a managed Hamlib setup,  Connect rig  starts the configured server when needed. You do not have to open Setup just to start it. The native Thetis choice uses its CAT connection directly. \n Set up a managed Hamlib connection \n \n In  Tools \u2192 Plot SWR , open  Hamlib Setup  and choose  Managed Hamlib server  as Backend. \n Beside  rigctld executable , click Browse and select the installed Hamlib program. Its location can differ from the example below. \n Press  Discover radios . Use  Find radio  to search, then select your radio in  Installed radio definitions . \n Enter the  Radio endpoint  used by your radio's CAT connection, or choose it from Detected serial ports. Match Serial baud / format to your radio or CAT software. \n Keep the local server port consistent with the Plot SWR connection. The example uses host 127.0.0.1 (this computer) and port 4532. \n Use  Start managed server , then  Test Rig Connection  to check the setup. Confirm that the displayed frequency agrees with your radio. This test is not a request to transmit. \n Close Setup and use  Connect rig  in Plot SWR. For later connections, Connect rig starts the configured managed server automatically when needed. \n \n Example Hamlib setup for PowerSDR/Thetis, supplied and approved by the owner. Select the model, port and speed for your station; COM21 is this example's CAT port, not the WN-2 USB connection. Select image to enlarge. \n Native PowerSDR/Thetis CAT is an alternative backend. For that choice, select the Native Thetis CAT port and baud; a managed Hamlib server is not used. An existing rigctld server instead uses its configured host and port. Do not copy a station-specific path or port without checking your own setup. \n Plan and run \n Select channels and start/end frequency, point count or step, and settling time. Choose how unavailable RF points should be handled. The operator controls transmission; the radio must allow frequency changes under the intended operating conditions. Start sweep reads the original frequency, tunes each point and checks the radio's reported frequency before recording. The app changes frequency only; it does not set PTT, TUNE, mode, power or antenna selection. Use a CAT connection that does not key the radio through serial control lines. Avoid split operation and another program retuning during the sweep. \n Real rig-assisted SWR sweep of Ray's 20M Yagi stack antennas, measured with WN-2 WNVSC7RK / CH 1. All 25 points are valid across 14.010\u201314.340 MHz. Select image to enlarge. \n The curve shows the measured antenna response during this sweep. The table retains each point's frequency, SWR, return loss, forward power and acquisition status. Your antenna and station readings will differ. \n Stop and restoration \n Stop prevents further points being scheduled. A command already sent cannot be recalled. If selected, restoration returns to the original frequency and checks readback. A lost connection can prevent restoration, so check the radio after a failure. Closing Plot SWR stops its own managed Hamlib process, not a separate server you started elsewhere. Use Manual sweep when assisted tuning is not appropriate for your station. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Multi-channel sweeps and comparison curves", "url": "comparison-curves.html", "text": "\n Multi-channel sweeps and comparison curves \n Multi-channel sweeps and comparison curves \n Compare channels in one run \n In Plot SWR, open the  CH  menu and select any combination of channels 1\u20134 on the chosen WN-2. Check the coupler and physical signal path for each. Start the manual or rig-assisted run as described in those topics. The channels share the frequency plan. This does not switch antennas or guarantee that every measurement was taken at exactly the same instant. \n Missing readings \n In Manual mode, Capture point advances only when all selected channels are valid. Partial results remain visible; retry or Skip point. In assisted mode, the chosen timeout policy decides whether missing channels are skipped or the run stops. \n Read and save the curves \n Colored keys identify each channel. The summary shows valid readings and minimum SWR by source/channel. Invalid sections break the trace rather than drawing a false zero. PNG, CSV and saved sweep files retain channel information. Open sweep replaces the current record set after the unsaved-data prompt; it does not merge arbitrary overlay files. Save separate runs with descriptive names and inspect them in turn. \n Select channels before acquisition; selection is locked while the sweep runs. Select image to enlarge. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "SWR protection, trips and manual reset", "url": "swr-protection.html", "text": "\n SWR protection, trips and manual reset \n SWR protection, trips and manual reset \n On this page Set protection for the intended device When a trip occurs Manual reset Optional automatic reset Set protection for the intended device \n Open  Device \u2192 SWR Protection . Select the WN-2 identity, check the supported command interpretation, choose the protection channel(s) and set the SWR limit. Use the interpretation appropriate to your WN-2; older hardware may support one selected channel rather than a channel combination.  Save preferences  keeps your choices.  Apply to device  sends the selected protection settings. Read the status afterward. The last successfully applied compatible settings are restored on a later connection; merely saving a new draft does not make it the applied setting. \n SWR Protection identifies the device, requested settings and reported trip state. Select image to enlarge. \n When a trip occurs \n A dedicated SWR Trip warning identifies the affected WN-2, regardless of which instruments are visible. Stop transmitting and address the cause before resetting. Resetting may restore an amplifier keying path connected through the relay. The warning is based on the WN-2's reported trip state. Unknown or disconnected is not the same as not tripped. Repeated reports of the same trip do not create a new dialog for every reading. \n Manual reset \n With the cause addressed and RF off, use  Reset SWR Relay  in the warning or protection window. Wait for the application to report that fresh WN-2 information shows not tripped. Sending the command alone is not confirmation of the relay state or its physical contacts. Dismiss warning closes the dialog without changing the relay. A new trip can reopen it. Use SWR warnings in the status area to return to warnings. \n Optional automatic reset \n Smart Reset  adds an optional RF-off waiting policy. It is OFF by default and is separate from the hardware trip threshold. Do not enable it without understanding what the relay controls in your station. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Smart Reset and protection restoration", "url": "smart-reset.html", "text": "\n Smart Reset and protection restoration \n Smart Reset and protection restoration \n On this page What Smart Reset does Enable it Why it may be waiting After the reset request What Smart Reset does \n Smart Reset can request a relay reset after a reported SWR trip when the WN-2 has shown RF off for five continuous seconds. It is optional and OFF by default. RayWn2App must remain running and receiving valid readings. \n Enable it \n Open  Device \u2192 SWR Protection , select the correct WN-2, and check  Enable Smart Reset after 5 seconds RF-off . Save the preference. Apply the intended protection settings to the device if they have not already been applied. \n Automatic reset is a separate choice from the SWR channel and limit. Select image to enlarge. \n Why it may be waiting \n All four channels' forward/reflected average and peak readings must remain below 0.1 W. Turning off RF on only the tripped channel is not enough if another channel is still reporting power. The warning identifies channels preventing the countdown. Stale, invalid or disconnected readings cannot establish RF off. A new RF reading restarts the waiting period. Another WN-2's readings do not determine this device's reset. \n After the reset request \n The application waits for fresh not-tripped information before showing confirmation. It does not assume a successful send means the relay reset. A failed reset or rapid repeat trips can require  Re-arm Smart Reset ; investigate the cause first. The software cannot guarantee the physical relay contacts or correct an antenna problem. Automatic reset may restore amplifier keying, so choose it only when appropriate for the connected station. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Overdrive warnings and hardware limits", "url": "overdrive.html", "text": "\n Overdrive warnings and hardware limits \n Overdrive warnings and hardware limits \n Set an amplifier-input warning \n Open  Device \u2192 Overdrive Warning . Select the WN-2 and the channel measuring amplifier input. Choose forward peak or forward average, enter a suitable limit in watts, and enable/save the warning. Choose a limit appropriate to your amplifier. The initial number is a starting value for editing, not a recommended limit. Dashboard scale colors and peak hold do not set this warning. \n Respond to a warning \n A fresh reading above the limit opens a prominent warning showing the device, channel, selected measurement, limit and reading. Reduce drive and check the station. Acknowledge closes the warning but leaves monitoring active. The status-bar Overdrive Warnings button reopens it. The warning rearms after readings fall sufficiently below the limit for the recovery period. Stale or invalid data does not prove recovery. Other WN-2 devices keep their own independent warning settings. \n What it does not do \n This is an application warning. It does not reduce transmitter power, operate PTT or provide the hardware's separate power-limit function. Use the amplifier's required protection and operating precautions regardless of the application warning. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Email setup, rules and configuration transfer", "url": "email.html", "text": "\n Email setup, rules and configuration transfer \n Email setup, rules and configuration transfer \n On this page Open Email Notifications Mail server tab Test delivery first Device rules tab Delivery policy tab Move settings to another computer Missing alerts checklist Open Email Notifications \n Choose  Device \u2192 Email Notifications . RayWn2App sends messages through the SMTP mail server you configure. It does not require a separate notification-service subscription. Keep the application running for monitoring and delivery. \n Mail server tab \n Enter the server, port, username, password/app password, sender email and recipients supplied by your mail provider. Select the required encrypted connection type. Common choices are STARTTLS on port 587 or TLS from connection start on port 465; follow your provider's instructions. \n Mail server setup. Use your own provider's account details. Select image to enlarge. \n Passwords are saved using your operating system's secure password storage. A blank password field reuses the saved password for the selected server/account. A different account needs its own password. Forget saved password removes the saved credential and stops monitoring until configured again. Some providers require an app password. Accounts that permit only browser-based OAuth sign-in are not supported by this setup. If delivery fails, check the provider's SMTP instructions and the reported error. \n Test delivery first \n Choose  Save and send test email  and check the recipient's mailbox, including junk mail. Server acceptance means the mail server accepted the message; it does not prove it reached the inbox. \n Device rules tab \n Select the WN-2 identity and check  Notify for this device . Global email enable alone is not enough. Choose the required trip, connection, RF, analog or PTT/low-RF rules. Save after editing. \n Select the device and enable its rules before expecting fault messages. Select image to enlarge. \n An SWR protection-trip notice is immediate. An ordinary SWR-above-limit rule uses the configured delay. Trip messages include channel/SWR information when available; the application does not invent a channel when the hardware report does not identify it. Analog low/high rules use that device's configured analog value and units. They do not automatically use a Temperature instrument's \u00b0C/\u00b0F preference. Match the units deliberately. PTT-gated low-RF rules require a correctly configured digital PTT input and RF channel. \n Delivery policy tab \n Set the threshold/loss delay, optional repeat notices and recovery notices. For emulator practice, also enable simulated/replay devices; leave it off for ordinary physical-station monitoring if you do not want practice alerts. \n Delays and repeats help control the number of messages. Select image to enlarge. \n Messages are rate limited and temporary delivery failures have bounded retries. Old pending messages eventually expire. Invalid or stale readings do not establish recovery. Read the Application Log if an expected message does not arrive. \n Move settings to another computer \n JSON Export saves the email configuration without passwords. JSON Import loads a draft; review it, select the correct device identities and save deliberately. Imported notifications start disabled so importing a file does not unexpectedly activate mail. Thunderbird Autoconfig XML import can fill supported outgoing-mail settings from a provider's file. It does not configure your station's device rules or import a password. Review the chosen server, encryption, username and sender before saving. \n Missing alerts checklist \n Check global enable, Notify for this device, the selected rule and delay, the device identity, current valid readings and the simulator-test option where relevant. A successful test email proves the mail path, not that a particular device rule is enabled. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Tone generator, pulses and analyzer coordination", "url": "tones.html", "text": "\n Tone generator, pulses and analyzer coordination \n Tone generator, pulses and analyzer coordination \n On this page Play a tone Pulsed and low-duty-cycle operation Coordinate with an analyzer Other audio tools Play a tone \n Open  Tools \u2192 Tone Generator . Select the audio output connected to your intended listening or radio-audio path. Set Tone #1 and Tone #2 frequencies, durations and amplitude. Choose Tone #1, Tone #2 or Both. Stop ends playback. \n Tone Generator controls audio playback; it does not key your transmitter. Select image to enlarge. \n Begin at a modest amplitude and use the station's normal audio-level checks. The output selection is an audio device on the computer, not a WN-2 channel. Changing devices or stopping the application ends the audio operation. \n Pulsed and low-duty-cycle operation \n Choose the pulsed option and set the on/off timing needed for your test. The duty cycle describes how much of each cycle contains tone. Check timing and duration before starting. A low duty cycle reduces average audio activity; it does not guarantee a safe RF power level. \n Coordinate with an analyzer \n Use the analyzer-coordination controls to choose the connected WN-2, sensor and Scope or Envelope Spectrum. Start the coordinated operation to play the selected stimulus and collect the specified short capture sequence. The application does not turn on RF. Establish the intended radio/audio path yourself. Captures are not precisely synchronized to each pulse edge, so do not interpret them as a triggered RF burst test. \n Other audio tools \n Tone Generator, Audible RF Monitor and Audio Messages coordinate so competing application audio operations do not keep playing over one another. Use Stop if you are uncertain which operation is active. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Audible SWR tuning and spoken readings", "url": "spoken-readings.html", "text": "\n Audible SWR tuning and spoken readings \n Audible SWR tuning and spoken readings \n Open Audible RF Monitor \n Choose  Tools \u2192 Audible RF Monitor . Select the WN-2 and RF channel. Choose the required audio behavior and output, then enable it deliberately. \n Source selection and audio settings for audible monitoring. Select image to enlarge. \n Tune by ear \n Lower pitch indicates lower SWR; the tuning sound changes with the reading, letting you listen while adjusting the station. Use it alongside the displayed SWR and power. Low RF, invalid or stale data is not a valid tuning measurement. \n Spoken readings \n Enable the desired spoken power/SWR readings and choose the available speech settings. The computer's selected voice/output may differ from the tuning-tone output. Check the volume and destination before a contest or a recording session. For a single reading without opening this window, use  Tools \u2192 Speak selected WN-2 \u2192 Channel 1\u20134 . The shortcut uses the device selected in the main toolbar. See  Station identity and shortcuts . Stop ends the audible operation. The feature does not control transmitter PTT or adjust your station. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Stored messages, recording and global hotkeys", "url": "audio-messages.html", "text": "\n Stored messages, recording and global hotkeys \n Stored messages, recording and global hotkeys \n On this page Prepare your messages Record and save Compact contest panel Global hotkeys Prepare your messages \n Open  Tools \u2192 Audio Messages , then  Settings . Each of the six slots has a button name, WAV file and play/record shortcut. Choose a meaningful name such as CQ or Exchange. Select the playback output connected to your intended audio path and set the playback volume. Choose a microphone and recording folder if you want to record new messages. \n Message names, files, recording controls and shortcuts are together in Settings. Select image to enlarge. \n Record and save \n Click the slot's  Record / Save  control to start recording. Speak the message, then press it again or Stop to finish and save. The new WAV file becomes that slot's message. Earlier files are retained, so a new recording does not overwrite your previous take. Choose WAV assigns an existing file. Clearing a slot's assignment does not delete the file. A recording ends automatically at the time limit shown in the window. Check the saved message by playing it before using it on air. \n Compact contest panel \n Close Settings to return to the small button panel. Click a named button to play it; Stop ends playback. The panel can be minimized or hidden while you use a logger. \n Compact message buttons leave room for other station software. Select image to enlarge. \n Global hotkeys \n Enable  Global hotkeys  if you want the shortcuts to work while another application has focus. Defaults are F2\u2013F7 for playback, Shift+F2\u2013Shift+F7 for recording and F8 for Stop/save. F1 opens Help. The previous complete factory shortcut set is shifted automatically; customized assignments are retained. A custom plain-F1 assignment is inactive until you choose another key in Settings. Change them in Settings to avoid conflicts with your logger. Registered keys are used by RayWn2App. Check the reported hotkey status; another application can prevent a key from being registered. With global hotkeys off, local keys work only while the message panel has focus. Hiding the panel does not disable enabled global hotkeys; quitting RayWn2App releases them. Global-key availability depends on the operating system and desktop. If reported unavailable, use the panel buttons or local keys. The application does not key the transmitter; use your established radio-audio/PTT arrangement. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Antenna switch panel and band codes", "url": "antenna-switch.html", "text": "\n Antenna switch panel and band codes \n Antenna switch panel and band codes \n On this page Open the compact panel Two-bank operation Band-code operation Before switching Open the compact panel \n Choose  Tools \u2192 Antenna Switch  and select your WN-2. Open Setup to choose the operating mode and names. The panel supports configurable external logic-output controllers; it is not a promise of plug-and-play compatibility with every antenna switch. \n Antenna Switch panel. Confirm the external controller and mapping before enabling physical control. Select image to enlarge. \n Two-bank operation \n Two banks can each select one of four named feeds through a compatible two-bit decoder. Bank 1 uses OUT1/OUT2; Bank 2 uses OUT3/OUT4. Setup assigns the output code for each feed. Use the actual controller's wiring and polarity requirements, not an assumed default mapping. \n Band-code operation \n Four-bit band-code mode uses all four outputs together. Set the names and codes required by your controller. The default set covers the usual amateur bands plus None. Selection is manual; it does not follow the radio's frequency automatically or retune it. Changing mode requires reviewing physical authorization again. The same outputs cannot simultaneously serve independent accessories with conflicting requirements. \n Before switching \n Stop transmitting. The panel waits for fresh, low-RF readings before allowing a change. Lost or invalid data blocks switching. This software check is not a substitute for the external controller's RF inhibit and relay sequencing. Do not connect relay coils directly to WN-2 logic outputs. Enable physical operation only after verifying the interface and mapping. Button selection represents the last transmitted code, not measured antenna position. Reconnection initializes outputs OFF, which may select a feed depending on the external decoder. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Rotator control availability", "url": "rotators.html", "text": "\n Rotator control availability \n Rotator control availability \n Rotator control availability \n This version does not provide RT-1/RT-2 rotator movement controls. Do not use generic digital-output or antenna-switch settings as a substitute for a verified rotator controller. Use the controller intended for your rotator. A station layout can still display WN-2 measurements while you operate separate rotator software or hardware. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "MQTT telemetry, credentials and startup", "url": "mqtt.html", "text": "\n MQTT telemetry, credentials and startup \n MQTT telemetry, credentials and startup \n On this page What MQTT provides Set up publishing Automatic startup Reading status remotely What MQTT provides \n MQTT lets RayWn2App send readings to a station server, dashboard or automation system. A broker is the server that receives and distributes those messages. You do not need MQTT for local WN-2 operation. RayWn2App publishes measurements and status. It does not accept incoming MQTT commands to operate the transmitter, protection relay or outputs. \n Set up publishing \n \n Connect the WN-2 you intend to monitor. \n Open  Tools \u2192 MQTT Telemetry . \n Enter the broker address, port and connection security required by your server. \n Enter your broker username/password if required and choose the publishing settings. \n Save and start publishing. Check the status, then confirm readings in your receiving dashboard. \n \n MQTT setup uses the broker information supplied by your station administrator. Select image to enlarge. \n Passwords can be kept in the operating system's secure password storage. Leave the password blank to reuse the saved credential for that server/account. Do not put passwords in a layout or share them in screenshots. \n Automatic startup \n Start publishing automatically when RayWn2App opens  is optional and defaults OFF. Enable and Save if wanted. It starts publishing the saved configuration; it does not automatically connect a WN-2. Unavailable data remains unavailable until the device has fresh readings. Stop ends the current publishing session. Clear the startup option and Save to prevent it starting next time. \n Reading status remotely \n A receiving dashboard should check fresh/unavailable status as well as numeric values. A stopped device or a network failure must not leave an old reading looking live. The supplied  Node-RED examples  provide station status, warnings and history. The optional compatibility flow is for dashboards expecting Dave Ginsberg's WaveNode-to-MQTT format. It is separate from the normal RayWn2App format and requires deliberate source selection. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Node-RED dashboards, trends and warnings", "url": "node-red.html", "text": "\n Node-RED dashboards, trends and warnings \n Node-RED dashboards, trends and warnings \n On this page What Node-RED does Station dashboard Station-health warnings Trends and event history Compatibility sample Troubleshooting What Node-RED does \n Node-RED runs on a station computer and can display readings received through your MQTT broker. A flow is a saved arrangement of steps that receives, processes and displays messages. The sample flows are optional; local RayWn2App operation does not depend on them. Have Node-RED and the broker configured before importing the supplied RayWn2App examples. Preserve existing flows and enter your own broker/security settings. Do not expose an unprotected station dashboard to the Internet. \n Station dashboard \n Open the configured Node-RED address followed by  /station  in your browser. Each device card identifies its source, physical/simulated state, readings and freshness. Check the identity before comparing it with the main application.  UNAVAILABLE / STALE  means the card has no current valid data. It is not zero RF.  Clear unavailable devices  hides those cards while keeping live devices. Repeated unavailable messages should not immediately recreate a cleared card; it returns when usable live data returns. \n Station-health warnings \n The dashboard can display the published station-health information and its configured warning rules. Review the device, condition and timestamp. Acknowledging a dashboard warning does not reset the WN-2 relay or correct the station condition. Rules and display state are separate from RayWn2App's native email and SWR protection settings. Keep the distinction clear when configuring alerts. \n Trends and event history \n From /station, open  Measurement trends and station events , or  /station/history . Select the publisher/device stream and channel. The view plots recent RF/DC readings and shows observed station events. Missing data breaks traces instead of drawing a false zero. The history is bounded and is not a permanent station log. Export selected history as JSON or measurements as CSV before restarting or redeploying the flow. Clear selected history affects that stream's history, not live device control. \n Compatibility sample \n The optional compatibility adapter translates a selected source to the message format expected by WaveNode-to-MQTT consumers. Dave's original dashboard is a separate third-party example. The RayWn2App-compatible sample adds clear unavailable/stale indications; its page is  /wavenode-sample . Choose the intended publisher, WN-2 and channel mapping. Do not merge multiple devices into one unidentified legacy stream. Preserve the native /station dashboard when adding a compatibility example. \n Troubleshooting \n First check that RayWn2App reports publishing active. Then check broker connection/security and the flow's selected source. A working browser page does not prove new MQTT messages are arriving. Confirm timestamps and status before using a reading. These dashboards monitor data; the supplied flows do not provide WN-2 hardware control. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Direct Thetis telemetry", "url": "thetis.html", "text": "\n Direct Thetis telemetry \n Direct Thetis telemetry \n On this page Send WN-2 readings directly to Thetis Configure Thetis reception Add the readings to a Thetis container Automatic startup Freshness and stopped data Send WN-2 readings directly to Thetis \n RayWn2App can publish one selected WN-2 channel directly to Thetis. MQTT is not required. Connect the desired WN-2 first, then open  Tools \u2192 Thetis Telemetry . \n Configure Thetis reception \n \n In Thetis, open  Setup \u2192 Serial/Network/Midi CAT \u2192 Multi Meter I/O . \n Add a UDP connection. For both programs on the same computer, use  127.0.0.1:9388 . \n Select direction  In , format  XML , terminator  NONE , and enable the connection. \n In RayWn2App, select the matching destination, WN-2 identity and RF channel, then  Save and start . \n \n Select the channel to publish here; a Thetis container number does not select a WN-2 channel. Select image to enlarge. \n UDP is a way to send data across the local network. Use a trusted network for a separate Thetis computer, with matching destination settings. This connection does not key or tune the radio. \n Add the readings to a Thetis container \n In Thetis  Setup \u2192 Appearance \u2192 Meters/Gadgets , select your container. Choose  Text Overlay , click the right arrow to add it, then select the overlay to edit it. Enter the desired text in both  RX Text  and  TX Text  if it should appear in receive and transmit. The  %  button opens the variable picker. Select the incoming WN-2 fields using the connection ID shown in your own Variables table. Useful fields are  peakpwr  for forward peak watts,  refpeakpwr  for reflected peak watts,  swr ,  device ,  channel  and  status . Average watts are  fwdpwr  and  refpwr . Include status so unavailable data is not mistaken for live data. Enable Show RX/Show TX for the container, then Apply/OK. If it is off-screen, use Recover Container. Thetis's ordinary internal power meters do not automatically become WN-2 meters merely by adding them. \n Automatic startup \n Enable  Start publishing automatically when RayWn2App opens  and Save if wanted. This starts the saved publishing configuration, not the WN-2 connection. Until the source connects, unavailable status is sent. Stop ends this session; clear the startup option to prevent the next automatic start. \n Freshness and stopped data \n Normal Stop/shutdown sends a stopped status. A crash or network failure can prevent it arriving, and Thetis may retain an old value. Check incoming timestamps/status and use a receiver-side timeout where configured. Do not use an old retained number as a current station reading. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Remote station viewing", "url": "remote-access.html", "text": "\n Remote station viewing \n Remote station viewing \n Monitoring away from the main window \n Use the Compact Station Panel or detached meters on the same computer. For another computer or browser, MQTT/Node-RED can display published measurements. Direct Thetis publishing serves a separate supported radio-software display. \n Monitoring is not remote control \n The telemetry features do not accept commands to key a transmitter, reset protection or switch WN-2 outputs. If you use remote-desktop software to operate the application, that is a separate connection with its own access and station-control responsibilities. Keep remote access restricted to trusted users and check data freshness. A dashboard displaying a number does not prove the station is currently connected. See  MQTT ,  Node-RED  or  Thetis  for setup steps. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Raw Data Emulator and multiple instances", "url": "emulator.html", "text": "\n Raw Data Emulator and multiple instances \n Raw Data Emulator and multiple instances \n On this page Practice without hardware Change the simulated readings Scope and protection practice More than one emulator Power, pause and close Practice without hardware \n The WN-2 Raw Data Emulator is a simulated meter for learning layouts and testing displays. Its readings are generated in the computer, not measured RF. \n \n Open  Diagnostics \u2192 WN-2 Raw Data Emulator . \n In the main device list, select  VIRTUAL-WN2-EMULATOR  and press  Connect . \n Assign your practice instruments to that exact Device identity. \n Look for Live and an increasing telemetry-frame count in the emulator window. Opening its window or checking Power ON is not a separate Connect action. Connect each emulator separately, just as you connect physical devices separately. \n \n Simulated RF controls for each coupler. Select image to enlarge. \n Change the simulated readings \n On RF couplers, enable the desired channels and change Forward ADC, reflected percentage and peak multiplier. ADC counts are the emulator's raw input level; the application converts them through the selected coupler settings. Reflected percentage is a power percentage. On DC / analog / digital, change input values to exercise those instruments. Digital loopback can make simulated outputs appear at the simulated inputs. None of these controls operates a physical WN-2. \n Practice voltage, temperature and digital input displays here. Select image to enlarge. \n Scope and protection practice \n Enable a Scope source and choose waveform settings. The normal WN-2 simulation uses nominal 40 kS/s; the optional 48 kS/s synthetic source is a separate test choice. It is not the computer audio output. \n Simulated waveform controls. Select image to enlarge. \n Configure and Apply protection to the virtual device before expecting a threshold trip. The protection tab also provides trip/reset test controls. To test Smart Reset, remove simulated RF on every channel that is preventing the five-second countdown. \n Protection test controls avoid needing repeated physical high-SWR conditions. Select image to enlarge. \n More than one emulator \n Choose  New emulator  to create another identity, such as VIRTUAL-WN2-EMULATOR-2. Select it in the toolbar and Connect. Reassign copied instruments to it. Each instance has independent stimuli and settings. \n Power, pause and close \n Power OFF simulates loss; ON makes the device available again. Automatic recovery must be enabled if you want it to reconnect after loss. Pause data simulates stale readings. Closing an emulator window disconnects that instance; minimize it to keep it running while working elsewhere. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Packet Monitor and traffic diagnostics", "url": "packet-monitor.html", "text": "\n Packet Monitor and traffic diagnostics \n Packet Monitor and traffic diagnostics \n Open Live Packet Monitor \n Choose  Diagnostics \u2192 Live Packet Monitor  and select the WN-2. This is a troubleshooting view of the information passing between the meter and application; ordinary operators do not need it open to read power or SWR. \n Tabs and meanings \n Telemetry RX shows normal incoming measurement data. Command TX shows commands sent by the application. Scope RX shows captured waveform data. Hover over a data field for an explanation of its meaning. \n Incoming readings from the connected physical WN-2. Select image to enlarge. \n Outgoing command information. A successful send is not physical relay feedback. Select image to enlarge. \n Scope data is separate from ordinary meter readings. Select image to enlarge. \n Pause and inspect \n Pause the monitor's display when you need to inspect a value. This does not necessarily pause the WN-2 or another capture window. Resume to see updates. The connection diagnostics help distinguish missing incoming data from a problem in an individual instrument's assignment. If support requests packet information, include the selected device, connection method and the action that led to the problem. Avoid sharing unrelated station logs or account details. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "Application Log and troubleshooting", "url": "application-log.html", "text": "\n Application Log and troubleshooting \n Application Log and troubleshooting \n On this page Read the Application Log Save information for support Logging settings Quick checks Read the Application Log \n Choose  Diagnostics \u2192 Application Log . The log records useful activity such as connections, warnings, recording errors and delivery status. Start here when a feature reports a problem but you do not know why. Use filters and search to narrow the entries. Follow keeps the newest activity in view; Pause lets you read without the list moving. Refresh reloads the view. The detailed/raw view is available when support needs the exact record, but is not required for ordinary troubleshooting. \n Save information for support \n Use Export to save relevant records. Include what you were doing and the time of the problem. Folder access opens the log location. Clear is a deliberate log operation; save anything you need first. \n Logging settings \n Review logging size/retention and health settings in the viewer's settings controls. Connection traffic recording is separate and optional. Turn it on only when useful for diagnosis; normal application logging can remain available without recording every packet. If storage fails, check disk space and folder access, then use the available retry option. A healthy meter display does not prove a log file is being written successfully. \n Quick checks \n For missing readings, check the selected device and instrument assignment. For email, check both global and per-device enable. For a stale remote dashboard, check publishing and timestamps. For capture problems, check source, Start/Pause state and clipping messages before changing unrelated settings. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}, {"title": "About, licensing and Help scope", "url": "about.html", "text": "\n About, licensing and Help scope \n About, licensing and Help scope \n About RayWn2App \n RayWn2App is an independent multi-OS application from RF StateSide for operating and displaying WaveNode WN-2 measurements. Open  Help \u2192 About  for the installed version, licensing and acknowledgments. \n Getting assistance \n When asking for support, include your operating system, the version shown in About, your WN-2 connection method and a short description of the steps leading to the problem. A screenshot of the relevant window is often helpful. Do not include mail or MQTT passwords. Save a copy of an affected layout before making further changes. The Application Log can provide useful details when requested. \n Help home  \u00b7 RF StateSide \u00b7 RayWn2App operator guide \n \n"}];